    {
      "calls": [
        {
          "ID": "toolu_01Ug1Zbf62w7RaPBrDugAJ1Q",
          "Name": "submit_read",
          "Input": {
            "assessments": [
              {
                "facts": [
                  "S1:F7",
                  "F26"
                ],
                "ref": "N1",
                "statement": "On the UK government's own account, the reciprocal energy ceasefire Trump announced on 11 October had not stopped Russian strikes on Ukraine's energy infrastructure two days later.",
                "supersedes": ""
              },
              {
                "facts": [
                  "S1:F2",
                  "S1:F7",
                  "S2:F6",
                  "S6:F21",
                  "S6:F22",
                  "S7:F29",
                  "F30"
                ],
                "ref": "N2",
                "statement": "London's response to the US–Russia agreement and energy ceasefire was to add pressure on Moscow and support for Kyiv rather than ease off, so the UK is running its own track alongside Washington's diplomacy, not waiting on it.",
                "supersedes": ""
              },
              {
                "facts": [
                  "S8:F31",
                  "S9:F35",
                  "S11:F37",
                  "F57",
                  "S13:F51",
                  "S14:F56",
                  "F55"
                ],
                "ref": "N3",
                "statement": "The week's domestic announcements share a regional-industrial pattern under the new Prime Minister, with public money and institutions placed outside London and Westminster: rail in Derby, buses in Falkirk, joint defence colleges and No10 North with Scotland.",
                "supersedes": ""
              }
            ],
            "statements": [
              {
                "cites": [
                  "L1",
                  "F55"
                ],
                "text": "This week's Stories are best read as the first full autumn of a new government: Andy Burnham has been Prime Minister since 20 July 2026, and he says he came into Downing Street wanting to do politics differently."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S1:F2",
                  "S1:F7"
                ],
                "text": "Abroad, the big shift came from Washington: the US and Russia reached an agreement on 9 October, and President Trump announced a reciprocal energy ceasefire on 11 October."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "F26",
                  "N1"
                ],
                "text": "But two days later the UK government was still describing Russian bombardment of Kyiv and of Ukraine's energy infrastructure. On London's own account, the ceasefire had not yet taken hold."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S2:F6",
                  "S6:F22",
                  "S7:F29",
                  "F23",
                  "F30",
                  "S6:F21"
                ],
                "text": "Britain's answer was to push harder, not to wait. A new sanctions package now covers over 90% of Russia's oil-producing capacity. Britain is sending £45 million of surface-to-air missiles, plus another package of Lightweight Multirole Missiles on top of the £1.6 billion already committed. And ministers have been asked to draw up more aid, sanctions and fiscal packages before winter."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "N2"
                ],
                "text": "Taken together, this suggests London is following its own course next to Washington's diplomacy rather than handing that diplomacy the lead on Ukraine."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S8:F31",
                  "S8:F34",
                  "S9:F35",
                  "S11:F37",
                  "S10:F36"
                ],
                "text": "At home, the rail Stories meet in one city. The Great British Railways headquarters for up to 450 staff, the first of up to 600 rail apprentices and a £1 billion northern train fleet built at Alstom's site are all in Derby, and fares have been frozen as services move into public ownership."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "F57",
                  "S13:F51",
                  "S14:F56",
                  "N3"
                ],
                "text": "The Scottish Stories fit the same pattern. The Prime Minister points to Greater Manchester's buses being built in Falkirk, offers Holyrood joint working through No10 North, and has won Scottish matching of £10 million for two defence colleges. It looks like a deliberate habit of putting public money and institutions in the regions, and that is worth watching as the 20 November UK-EU summit approaches."
              }
            ],
            "stories": []
          }
        }
      ],
